---
import Image from '../assets/og/social-paper.webp';
import Article from '../components/article/Article.astro';
import ArticleAside from '../components/article/ArticleAside.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../components/article/ArticleHeader.astro';
import ArticleListItem from '../components/article/ArticleListItem.astro';
import Conclusion from '../components/article/Conclusion.astro';
import ExternalLink from '../components/article/ExternalLink.astro';
import InternalLink from '../components/article/InternalLink.astro';
import H2 from '../components/article/H2.astro';
import H3 from '../components/article/H3.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../components/article/TableOfContents.astro';
import U from '../components/U.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const image_url = new URL(Image.src, Astro.site);

const description =
  'The Social Paper examines the architectural and economic forces that transformed the World Wide Web from a decentralized network of human connection into an engine of surveillance capitalism. It proposes needed steps for ensuring user privacy.';

const parts = [
  {
    id: 'part-i',
    part: 'Part I',
    title: 'The Architecture of Openness and the Lack of Privacy Primitives',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-ii',
    part: 'Part II',
    title: 'Hooked on the Feed and the Commodification of Connection',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-iii',
    part: 'Part III',
    title: 'The Next Generation: Easing Children into the Digital World',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-iv',
    part: 'Part IV',
    title: 'The Automation of Outrage and Reclaiming the Human Network',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-v',
    part: 'Part V',
    title: 'The Corporate Guest in a Human Space',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-vi',
    part: 'Part VI',
    title: 'Law, Not Policy — Free Speech and Platform Responsibility',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-vii',
    part: 'Part VII',
    title: 'Lack of Accountability and Ethics in the Corporate Space',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-viii',
    part: 'Part VIII',
    title: 'The Blueprint for a Private Web',
  },
];

const sp = 'Social Paper';
const spSection = (id: string) => {
  const part = parts.find((p) => p.id === id);
  if (!part) throw new Error(`Unknown part: ${id}`);
  return `${part.part}: ${part.title}`;
};
---

<Layout size="large">
  <Fragment slot="header">
    <title>Social Paper &mdash; The Civia Project</title>

    <meta name="description" content={description} />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={image_url} />

    <!-- X Card -->
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={image_url} />
  </Fragment>

  <Article>
    <ArticleHeader
      label="White Paper"
      title="The Social Paper"
      authors={['Alexandru Ifrim', 'Gabriel Pene']}
      date="2026-06-22"
      draft
    />

    <ArticleAside label="Abstract" italic>
      <p>
        <em>The Social Paper</em> examines the architectural and economic forces that
        transformed the World Wide Web from a decentralized network of human connection
        into an engine of surveillance capitalism. It proposes needed steps for ensuring
        user privacy.
      </p>
      <p>
        By tracing how privacy primitives were never embedded as non-negotiable
        defaults in the social web—and how even strong transit and messaging
        encryption leaves users exposed to the platforms they interact with—this
        document dissects how commercial social media platforms have exploited
        the Web's open DNA to prioritize algorithmic addiction, ad-driven
        capital gains, and corporate dominance over users' benefit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Furthermore, it addresses the systemic vulnerabilities that allow
        AI-driven bot networks to act as super-spreaders of propaganda,
        misinformation, disinformation, malinformation, and fake news, alongside
        the urgent need to design "safe by default" environments for the next
        generation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ultimately, this paper does not serve as a foundational blueprint, but as
        an exercise in reclaiming the digital town square. It proposes a radical
        architectural shift toward "local-only" privacy processing, cryptographic
        human-identity verification, and the establishment of a transparent,
        open-source, non-profit organization.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is a fundamentally rebuilt social web: one engineered for human
        well-being, where users are protected, companies are mere guests, and
        capital gains are permanently removed from the equation.
      </p>
    </ArticleAside>

    <TableOfContents items={parts} />

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-i" part="Part I">
        The Architecture of Openness and the Lack of Privacy Primitives
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-i-the-necessary-architecture-of-openness"
        >The Necessary Architecture of Openness</H3
      >
      <p>
        When Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web in 1989, his primary
        objective was to solve a specific problem at CERN: the fragmentation of
        information across incompatible computer systems. The Web was designed
        as a radically open, decentralized system for researchers to share
        documents. Its foundational building blocks—HTTP, HTML, and URLs—were
        engineered for frictionless connectivity and data retrieval, built atop
        an Internet whose own interconnection depended on Border Gateway
        Protocol (BGP) routing between Autonomous Systems (ASNs)—the
        independently operated networks of Internet Service Providers (ISPs) of
        different tiers.
      </p>

      <p>
        Noticeably absent from these foundational protocols were native privacy
        primitives. There was no built-in mechanism for identity verification,
        no native encryption, and no concept of personal data sovereignty.
        Crucially, the Internet was not built with privacy in mind—<em
          >nor should it have been</em
        >. Over 35 years ago, this radical openness was a mandatory feature. The
        cryptographic tools and computational power required for native privacy
        would have crushed the fragile early network. It had to be frictionless
        to allow for rapid, decentralized innovation.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, as the Internet transitioned from an academic network to a
        commercial engine, the absence of privacy layers paved the way for an
        ecosystem dominated by surveillance capitalism. That does not mean every
        service on the Web requires the same privacy primitives—requirements are
        business-specific. But wherever user behavior and interests can be
        extracted, privacy primitives must be required—and on the open web, that
        requirement can only be imposed legally, not enforced by the underlying
        protocols. Those extractions are routinely used to serve targeted ads and
        curated news—and in doing so, platforms can push users toward more
        polarizing content, even when no one set out to divide them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because the Internet had to remain open, privacy cannot be mandated at
        the protocol layer without closing the network to innovation. Requiring
        consumer protection on the social web is therefore a legislative
        endeavour—not a technical one. IPsec, TLS, WireGuard, and end-to-end
        encryption are real privacy primitives; they protect users from third
        parties and, when enforced, from platforms themselves. But on commercial
        social media they cannot be <em>technically enforced</em> as
        non-negotiable defaults—a platform can always decrypt at the destination,
        bury E2EE in settings, or remove it entirely. Only law can require that
        those primitives be implemented, maintained, and honored. Technical
        architecture shows what compliance <em>looks like</em>; legislation is
        what makes compliance <em>mandatory</em>.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-the-scarcity-of-privacy-primitives"
        >The Scarcity of Privacy Primitives</H3
      >
      <p>
        A "primitive" in computer science is a basic building block upon which
        more complex systems are built. While the Web developed robust
        primitives for communication and data transfer, and later added genuine
        privacy primitives that protect consumers from third parties. Yet for the
        social web, those primitives were never embedded as structural
        defaults—and they do not, on their own, protect users from the platforms
        at the other end of the connection:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Cookies (1994):</U> Originally introduced by Netscape to solve a technical
          issue (allowing a shopping cart to remember items), cookies were quickly
          weaponized by ad networks into the primary vector for third-party tracking—and,
          as cookies were later restricted by legislation (e.g. <ExternalLink
            href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32002L0058"
            >ePrivacy</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32016R0679"
            >GDPR</ExternalLink
          >) and browser policy, similar persistence shifted to localStorage, sessionStorage,
          and IndexedDB.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>IPsec (1995):</U> A genuine privacy primitive at the network
          layer—widely deployed in VPNs and enterprise security to protect
          communications from third-party adversaries on the path. It does not,
          however, protect users from the platform at the destination: social
          media services still decrypt and harvest the raw data when it arrives.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>SSL/TLS (HTTPS):</U> Introduced in the mid-1990s, this encrypts
          data in transit and protects consumers from eavesdropping by third
          parties on the network. At the platform termination point, the
          destination service still sees and can harvest the plaintext payload.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>WireGuard (2015):</U> Designed as a modern, high-performance
          alternative to older VPN protocols like IPsec, WireGuard simplified
          secure tunneling through state-of-the-art cryptography and a
          remarkably small, auditable codebase. Like IPsec, it is a valid
          primitive for securing transit from third parties; it does not prevent
          the ultimate destination (the social platform) from harvesting the
          decrypted payload once it arrives.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>End-to-End Encryption (E2EE):</U> True end-to-end encryption is a
          genuine privacy primitive: when enforced, it protects message content
          from third parties and from the platform itself. Commercial social
          media has historically treated it as optional, buried in settings, and
          revocable when revenue or regulation demands—never as a non-negotiable
          default.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Mobile:</U> The same scarcity played out on mobile: advertising identifiers
          (IDFA, GAID), on-device app storage, and third-party SDKs became primary
          vectors for behavioral extraction—met only later with restrictions like
          App Tracking Transparency.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Because privacy against platform extraction was not a structural
        primitive of the social web—and because even strong third-party
        protections were treated as optional features—commercial platforms had to
        bolt privacy onto the sides of their products. As the digital economy
        matured, platform architects realized that engineering true privacy into
        the system would directly cannibalize their primary source of capital:
        targeted ads.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
        >The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives</H3
      >
      <p>
        To fix this, the architecture must shift from centralized corporate
        servers to local processing—meaning the user's own hardware handles the
        computational load. True privacy primitives for the modern social web
        must be built entirely around a "local-only" framework that securely
        spans the user's personal hardware ecosystem. The primitives below are
        the technical expression of those legal obligations—the architecture a
        steward builds when privacy is required, not optional. This is a
        foundational architecture—not a finished privacy guarantee. Governance,
        query safeguards, and visible user control must still be woven in:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Local-only feed building:</U> At the platform level, public posts & content
          are processed with machine learning so they can be matched to what users
          are looking for, then stored in searchable databases on the platform. However,
          instead of a centralized server profiling the user to decide what they should
          see, this matching process is inverted. The user's device locally computes
          an "intent"—a secure, on-device representation of their current interests
          or search parameters—and sends it to the server to run the matching pipeline
          for that feed request. The server does not retain intent to build a profile,
          and it does not learn which content they engaged with from on-device interaction
          history.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Local-only tracking of user platform interaction:</U> The device locally
          observes user behavior to personalize the experience. If the platform needs
          to update its broader models, it must rely on volunteers helping it do so
          or by collecting anonymized data strictly from users who explicitly allow
          it.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Local-only settings:</U> By enforcing local-only settings, user preferences
          (blocks, themes, filters) act as an on-device firewall, preventing platforms
          from mining "privacy toggles" as just another psychological data point.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Encrypted Cross-Device Synchronization:</U> To ensure a seamless user
          experience, these local states are shared exclusively between the user's
          own active sessions across their phone, tablet, laptop, and PC. This creates
          a secure, personal data mesh. Synchronization happens via zero-trust, end-to-end
          encrypted relays or direct peer-to-peer connections. The platform's servers
          act only as blind couriers; they never hold the master copy, the decryption
          keys, or the plaintext data. <ExternalLink
            href="https://support.signal.org/hc/en-us/articles/213134507-Linked-Devices"
            >Signal</ExternalLink
          > and <ExternalLink
            href="https://faq.whatsapp.com/general/download-and-installation/about-linked-devices"
            >WhatsApp</ExternalLink
          > already demonstrate this for messaging: linked devices stay synchronized end-to-end encrypted across
          phone, tablet, and desktop.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Encrypted backups:</U> To protect against device loss without reverting
          to corporate cloud storage, local state is backed up end-to-end encrypted.
          Backup relays or user-controlled storage hold only ciphertext—the platform
          never holds the decryption keys or plaintext data. <ExternalLink
            href="https://support.signal.org/hc/en-us/articles/360007059732-Backup-and-Restore-Messages"
            >Signal</ExternalLink
          > and <ExternalLink
            href="https://faq.whatsapp.com/general/chats/how-to-back-up-to-google-drive-with-end-to-end-encryption"
            >WhatsApp</ExternalLink
          > have already proven this model for messaging backups—Signal on its own
          infrastructure, WhatsApp to Google Drive and iCloud when end-to-end
          encrypted backup is enabled; the same approach can extend to the broader
          social web.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-1" number={1} title="Improving the Social Web">
        <p>
          Improving the Social Web requires recognizing that while the foundational
          building blocks of the past cannot be changed, the DNA of the Internet
          is not static. To reclaim users' benefit and move beyond the era of
          surveillance capitalism, privacy can no longer be treated as a premium
          UI feature, an obscure setting, or a corporate promise. It must be
          woven directly into the Web's DNA.
        </p>

        <p>
          By demanding and implementing local-only feed building, local
          settings, local-only tracking of user platform interaction, encrypted
          cross-device synchronization, and encrypted backups, the tech industry
          can fundamentally break the centralized data-harvesting model while
          preserving connectivity.
        </p>

        <p>
          <ExternalLink
            href="https://support.signal.org/hc/en-us/articles/213134507-Linked-Devices"
            >Signal</ExternalLink
          > and <ExternalLink
            href="https://faq.whatsapp.com/general/download-and-installation/about-linked-devices"
            >WhatsApp</ExternalLink
          > have already proven encrypted cross-device synchronization for messaging; both have demonstrated encrypted backups
          as well—Signal on its own infrastructure, WhatsApp to Google Drive and
          iCloud when users enable <ExternalLink
            href="https://faq.whatsapp.com/general/chats/how-to-back-up-to-google-drive-with-end-to-end-encryption"
            >end-to-end encrypted backup</ExternalLink
          >. These are not
          untested ideas—the social web need only adopt what messaging has already
          demonstrated.
        </p>

        <p>
          Local-only processing is the necessary starting point, not the entire
          answer. Until privacy becomes a structural reality enforced by
          legislation, it will remain a fragile illusion.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-ii" part="Part II">
        Hooked on the Feed and the Commodification of Connection
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-ii-social-media-hooked-on-the-feed"
        >Social Media: Hooked on the Feed</H3
      >
      <p>
        Social media platforms generate revenue by showing you ads. It is their
        core engine. The absolute vast majority of their multi-trillion-dollar
        valuations comes from harvesting identity and selling attention. Paid
        services and features account for a very small percentage of their
        bottom line.
      </p>

      <p>
        In their early days, platforms like Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram
        focused purely on user acquisition and connection. They were
        venture-capital funded, and their investors needed those networks to
        reach critical mass—winning the network-effects race was the path to a
        successful exit. But once these companies went public, the stock market
        demanded perpetual growth in Average Revenue Per User (ARPU). To drive
        these capital gains, social media platforms made a critical architectural
        shift: they abandoned chronological timelines in favor of algorithmic
        feeds.
      </p>

      <p>
        The engineering mandate became simple—keep people hooked on the feed for
        as long as possible. The longer a user continuously scrolls, the more
        ads they can be shown. Independent analysis confirms that major
        platforms' algorithms reward engagement—and that outrage spreads before
        fact-checking can occur. In this environment, privacy and users' benefit
        are actively antithetical to the business model. Platforms designed user
        interfaces to encourage oversharing and perfectly tailor the addiction
        loop.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-ii-algorithmic-interventions">Algorithmic Interventions</H3>
      <p>
        The most direct way to fix filter bubbles is not to retune a platform's
        engagement objective function, but to restore chronological timelines by
        default and—when users explicitly opt in—let locally computed intent
        drive what they see.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>User intent over engagement rewards:</U> Platforms should not train
          algorithms to maximize clicks, likes, and watch time—or tune objective
          functions to serve a "balanced diet" they define unilaterally. When users
          explicitly choose an algorithmic feed, what they see should follow their
          own locally computed intent: a secure, on-device query sent to the server
          to run the matching pipeline for that request—without retaining intent
          to build a profile or optimizing for engagement behind the user's back.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>The Exploration vs. Exploitation Tradeoff:</U> Recommendation systems
          heavily favor "exploitation" (showing you what they know you like). To break
          echo chambers, systems need to dial up "exploration" (taking a risk by showing
          you something entirely new). This introduces planned serendipity into a
          user's feed.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Bridging-Based Ranking:</U> When users opt into algorithmic feeds,
          instead of promoting content that gets a high reaction from a specific,
          polarized cluster of users, ranking can recognize sentiment across
          diverse, opposing user clusters—not only positive reactions—and ensure
          the full spectrum of perspectives is served. This elevates content that
          finds common ground without silencing disagreement or over-indexing on
          outrage from any one side.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Conclusion
        id="conclusion-2"
        number={2}
        title="Adding the Social back in Social Media"
      >
        <p>
          For too long, the "social" in social media has been entirely
          overshadowed by the "media" of advertising. Social networks should not
          be vectors for capital gains, engineered solely to keep investors
          happy and stock prices climbing. Their fundamental purpose should be
          to serve as a vector for genuine human connection. The Internet was
          designed to be a global melting pot, a shared digital space where our
          differences are showcased to be cherished and celebrated. Instead, the
          current ad-driven models weaponize these differences, algorithmically
          optimizing for outrage to divide and polarize us simply because anger
          increases screen time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Commercial platforms stripped away users' benefit by abandoning
          chronological timelines and making outrage-optimized algorithmic feeds
          the default—and often the only—option. A healthier social network must
          restore that choice. Users should be able to select the feed that
          suits them: a reverse-chronological timeline by default, or a
          local-only algorithmic feed when they explicitly opt in. The algorithm
          must never be imposed; it must be a deliberate choice, with intent
          computed on the user's own devices and without surrendering engagement
          data to build a corporate behavioral profile.
        </p>

        <p>
          Platform-funded studies claiming that feed changes have little effect
          on polarization should be read with extreme skepticism. When the
          subject of the research funds and controls the experiment, buries
          opt-in privacy features, and alters algorithms mid-trial without
          disclosure, the conclusions serve the platform—not the public.
        </p>

        <p>
          To reclaim this original promise, the underlying ownership structures
          of these platforms must change. The digital town square should not be
          owned by a for-profit corporation beholden to the Stock Market.
          Instead, social media platforms should be stewarded by non-profit
          organizations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Furthermore, a social network's code should be open, not closed behind
          proprietary corporate walls. Open-source code allows a global
          community of developers, ethicists, and users to help the platform
          grow, audit its algorithms, and ultimately keep it honest. Only by
          combining non-profit stewardship, open-source transparency, and <InternalLink
            href="#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
            paper={sp}
            section={spSection('part-i')}
            heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
          /> can we finally add the "social" back into social media.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-iii" part="Part III">
        The Next Generation: Easing Children into the Digital World
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-iii-the-legislative-wave">The Legislative Wave</H3>
      <p>
        The addictive, ad-driven architecture of modern social media has proven
        especially catastrophic for the developing minds of children.
        Recognizing this, a massive legislative wave has recently swept the
        globe. Over the last two years, governments have shifted from issuing
        warnings to enacting hard legal barriers. <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024A00127/asmade/text"
          >Australia</ExternalLink
        > has been at the forefront: since 10 December 2025, children under 16
        have been barred from holding accounts on major platforms such as
        Instagram, TikTok, and YouTube, with fines of up to A$49.5 million
        levied on companies that fail to comply—while imposing no penalties on
        young people themselves. Nations ranging from <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/50/contents"
          >the UK</ExternalLink
        > and <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.assemblee-nationale.fr/dyn/17/dossiers/proteger_mineurs_reseaux_sociaux_17e"
          >France</ExternalLink
        > to <ExternalLink
          href="https://uaelegislation.gov.ae/en/legislations/4506"
          >the UAE</ExternalLink
        > are currently implementing their own age restrictions and mandatory
        parental consent laws.
      </p>

      <p>
        These laws come from a place of necessary urgency. However, as the <ExternalLink
          href="https://news.un.org/en/story/2025/12/1166557"
          >United Nations</ExternalLink
        > and <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-01-07/social-media-ban-mental-health-different-approach/104789108"
          >child psychology experts</ExternalLink
        > have pointed out, simply banning children from platforms is treating
        the symptom rather than curing the disease. <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.unicef.org.au/unicef-youth/staying-safe-online/social-media-ban-explainer"
          >UNICEF Australia</ExternalLink
        > agrees: the real fix is improving social media safety and listening
        to young people—not merely delaying their access. Enforcing these bans
        also pushes platforms toward behavioural inferencing and biometric age
        checks—the same profiling machinery this paper argues against.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of this criticism is an argument for ignoring the law. Where
        legislators have acted—as in <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024A00127/asmade/text"
          >Australia</ExternalLink
        > and the other jurisdictions named above—platforms operating in those
        markets must comply fully. This paper may disagree with ban-first or
        surveillance-heavy enforcement models and still hold that legal
        obligations are binding: disagreement with policy is not permission to
        disregard it.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-safe-by-design">Safe by Design</H3>
      <p>
        Completely isolating minors until their 16th birthday creates a
        dangerous cliff-edge scenario: a child goes from having zero digital
        exposure to being suddenly thrust into a highly manipulative,
        algorithmically hostile environment with no preparation. Because
        children are growing up in a digital-first world, they need to be
        carefully <em>eased</em> into social media, not just locked out of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Easing children into the digital town square means the spaces they
        inhabit must be "safe by design" from the ground up. This requires an
        entirely different set of rules for underage accounts. Children do not
        need algorithmic feeds optimized for endless scrolling; they need
        chronological feeds that allow them to step away naturally. They need
        the absolute guarantee, backed by <InternalLink
          href="#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
          paper={sp}
          section={spSection('part-i')}
          heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
        />, that their behavioral data is not being harvested to build psychological
        profiles for targeted advertising.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-guardrails-for-minor-accounts"
        >Guardrails for Minor Accounts</H3
      >
      <p>
        Safe architecture alone is not enough. Where age restrictions,
        parental-consent requirements, or other minor-protection laws apply—as
        they already do in <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024A00127/asmade/text"
          >Australia</ExternalLink
        >, <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/50/contents"
          >the UK</ExternalLink
        >, <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.assemblee-nationale.fr/dyn/17/dossiers/proteger_mineurs_reseaux_sociaux_17e"
          >France</ExternalLink
        >, <ExternalLink
          href="https://uaelegislation.gov.ae/en/legislations/4506"
          >the UAE</ExternalLink
        >, and beyond—the platform will respect them—regardless of whether we
        agree with every policy choice behind them. Compliance is not optional
        decoration; it is part of easing children in responsibly, rather than
        treating regulation and product design as opposing forces.
      </p>

      <p>
        Underage accounts operate under deliberate limitations on the social
        graph—and those limitations sit in parents' hands.
      </p>

      <p>
        Public accounts eligible for minor interaction must appear on <strong
          >curated allowlists</strong
        > vetted against child-safety criteria. The allowlist defines what is
        <em>eligible</em>, not what is automatic: parents still choose what
        their child may select from those lists.
      </p>

      <p>
        New connections—follows, direct messages, and other one-to-one or group
        links—require explicit <strong>validation from a verified parent or
        guardian</strong> before they take effect. A minor cannot freely expand
        their reach; every new link passes through a parent. Even when a public
        account is on the allowlist, a parent must still approve each specific
        connection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Guardrails on an underage account—connection rules, feed type, time
        limits, and other restrictions—can be <strong
          >added and removed by parents</strong
        >. The platform provides safe defaults; parents retain full control to
        tighten or relax limitations as their child matures.
      </p>

      <Conclusion
        id="conclusion-3"
        number={3}
        title="Protecting the Vulnerable"
      >
        <p>
          Protecting the next generation requires more than a single fix. We
          must enforce digital age verification so we actually know when a minor
          is using a platform. We must stop treating children as a lucrative
          data demographic. <InternalLink
            href="#part-iii-the-legislative-wave"
            paper={sp}
            section={spSection('part-iii')}
            heading="The Legislative Wave"
          />, we must respect it—even when we believe safe-by-design architecture would serve children better
          than ban-first approaches alone. And we must constrain the social graph
          itself—through curated allowlists of eligible public accounts—with
          parents choosing what their child may access from those lists—parent
          validation of every new connection, and parent-controlled limitations
          that can be added or removed as needed—so that easing in never means
          exposure without supervision.
        </p>

        <p>
          We need to provide them with digital literacy education built natively
          into the platforms themselves, teaching them how to navigate online
          spaces, spot misinformation, and curate their own digital well-being.
          By redesigning the architecture to be safe by default, we can ease
          kids into the social fabric of the Internet, ensuring they learn how
          to connect and thrive before they are exposed to the deeper
          complexities of the open Web.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-iv" part="Part IV">
        The Automation of Outrage and Reclaiming the Human Network
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-iv-the-ease-of-creating-and-managing-bots"
        >The Ease of Creating and Managing Bots</H3
      >
      <p>
        The radical openness that allowed the early Web to flourish has become
        the exact vulnerability currently destroying its social fabric. Because
        there is no structural identity verification on the modern web, creating
        and managing bots on social media has become trivially easy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Anyone with basic coding knowledge and a few dollars can spin up
        thousands of synthetic accounts using automated scripts and open APIs.
        Today, bots are not just spamming links; they are weaponized to game
        algorithmic feeds; they act as super-spreaders of propaganda,
        misinformation, disinformation, malinformation, and fake news. They
        exist to artificially amplify outrage and polarize the public.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-the-industry-of-social-media-farms"
        >The Industry of Social Media Farms</H3
      >
      <p>
        Bots are only half the problem. Across the globe, an entire industry of
        social media farms operates in plain sight: warehouses of phones,
        freelancers paid per engagement, and operators juggling dozens—or
        thousands—of accounts at once. These networks do not exist to converse.
        They exist to manufacture momentum. A single farm can swarm a post with
        likes and reposts until algorithms mistake manufactured hype for organic
        virality, pushing divisive content into millions of genuine feeds. The
        same infrastructure is used to spread propaganda, misinformation,
        disinformation, malinformation, and fake news: coordinated accounts
        amplify outrage, drown out nuance, and create the illusion that extreme
        positions dominate the public square.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unlike fully automated bots, farm accounts are often operated by real
        people on real devices, which makes them harder for trust-and-safety
        teams to classify and remove. They exploit the same open architecture
        and engagement-driven ranking that bots do—only at industrial scale.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-ai-powered-bots-and-farms">AI-Powered Bots and Farms</H3>
      <p>
        Generative AI has supercharged both threats at once. Scripted bots no
        longer need hand-written scripts, and farm operators no longer need
        rooms of people typing identical replies. The same models that draft
        marketing copy can now run entire account networks—holding contextual
        conversations, mimicking human empathy, and producing flawless, highly
        persuasive text at an infinite scale. A single operator can manage what
        used to require a warehouse.
      </p>

      <p>
        Beyond text, generative AI has ushered in a new era of synthetic
        media—hyper-realistic deepfakes, fabricated photos, and completely fake
        videos that are now nearly indistinguishable from reality, bypassing
        human skepticism and rewriting it in real time. Because these bots act
        and sound like real users, and can seamlessly generate fake visual
        evidence of events that never happened, they are becoming nearly
        impossible to spot through traditional means and incredibly difficult
        for trust and safety teams to remove. The cost of generating a million
        synthetic opinions, paired with fabricated visual media, is effectively
        zero, allowing bad actors to entirely drown out genuine human discourse.
        Defending democracies against this threat requires structural
        verification, deliberation, and accountability—not content moderation
        alone.
      </p>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-4" number={4} title="People First, Bots Never">
        <p>
          To successfully defend the digital town square, the hierarchy of a
          healthy social network must be absolute: <strong
            >people first, bots never.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <p>
          That requires every account to be linked to a verified digital
          identity—cryptographically tying the account to a real person, so
          there is always a human behind it. When digital presence is
          accountable, consequence-free anonymity and inauthentic amplification
          can no longer dominate the network. This is not a perfect solution,
          but it is a meaningful improvement over the system we have today.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-v" part="Part V"> The Corporate Guest in a Human Space </H2>

      <H3 id="part-v-rebalancing-the-current-power-dynamic"
        >Rebalancing the Current Power Dynamic</H3
      >
      <p>
        In the current iteration of the Web, users are treated as the product,
        and corporations are treated as the primary customers. Social media
        architectures are optimized entirely to appease these commercial
        entities, granting them unfettered access to human attention and data.
        To grab and hold that attention, engagement-driven ranking tends to
        amplify polarizing and inflammatory content—material that provokes
        strong reactions and keeps users on the app longer so that more ads can
        be served. The platforms decide the defaults: algorithmic feeds over
        chronological timelines, buried privacy settings over visible control,
        corporate data retention over user sovereignty. They own the social
        graph, the ranking systems, and the unilateral right to change the
        rules—often announced in a policy update long after the product has
        already shifted beneath users' feet.
      </p>

      <p>
        This imbalance is not a failure of goodwill; it is a structural
        consequence of for-profit stewardship. Public social media companies owe
        a fiduciary duty to shareholders, not to the people using the platform.
        Privacy primitives, chronological feeds, and reduced engagement are not
        neutral product choices—they are direct threats to Average Revenue Per
        User. A for-profit board cannot voluntarily surrender the architectural
        levers that drive capital gains and expect to remain in office. As long
        as the venue is built to maximize shareholder value, users will always
        sit at the bottom of the hierarchy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rebalancing this dynamic requires reversing who the space is for. In a
        human social network, power must flow from verified people outward.
        Corporations may participate in the conversation, but they do not
        architect the venue, own behavioral data, or decide by default what
        humans see in their feeds. That reversal is architectural and
        economic—not a settings panel, a transparency report, or a promise to
        "do better." The platform must be governed for community welfare first;
        only then can companies be relegated to guests rather than overlords.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-pure-capitalism-and-social-capitalism"
        >Pure Capitalism and Social Capitalism</H3
      >
      <p>
        These are not abstract ideologies—they are two operating models for how
        a social platform can be run. <em>Pure capitalism on the social web</em> is
        the dominant model today: revenue from advertising and attention harvesting,
        growth and ARPU as the measures of success, and user data and engagement treated
        as inputs to capital gains. Algorithmic feeds are tuned to maximize time-on-app.
        Encryption, privacy controls, and feed choice exist only so long as they do
        not threaten revenue—when they do, they are buried, made optional, or removed
        entirely. Surveillance capitalism, as described throughout this paper, is
        the mechanism; pure capitalism names the governance outcome: shareholder value
        placed above community welfare.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Social capitalism on the social web</em> inverts that priority. The platform's
        primary obligation is to its community—not to profit margins, quarterly earnings,
        or investor returns. Capital is still required to operate the platform. Revenue
        from optional premium features and services, donations, sponsorships, and
        grants must fund maintenance, research and development, salaries, and bonuses—but
        that capital does not own the architecture or the data layer. Stewardship
        by a non-profit organization is the structural guarantee that community needs,
        not capital needs, set the product direction. The platform's open-source code,
        released under a license separate from this website, will permit anyone to
        fork and operate a competing social media service. That ensures community
        obligations are enforceable—not merely asserted in marketing copy. If the
        steward breaks the rules, users are not locked in: they can audit the code,
        depart, and sustain the mission from a fork. Feeds are not optimized for capital
        gains; they are engineered for human connection and well-being.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Success metric:</U> Under pure capitalism, the platform optimizes for
          ARPU and shareholder return. Under social capitalism, it optimizes for user
          well-being and community health.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Steward:</U> A for-profit board accountable to investors versus a non-profit
          organization accountable to its users and the public.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Corporate role:</U> Owner of the venue, the graph, and the defaults—versus
          guest, present by contribution and subject to the community's rules.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Privacy primitives:</U> Negotiable the moment they threaten revenue—versus
          non-negotiable requirements—legally mandated and structurally implemented
          in the architecture.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The distinction is not rhetorical; it determines what can be built and
        what will survive. Under pure capitalism, every privacy primitive described in <InternalLink
          href="#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
          paper={sp}
          section={spSection('part-i')}
          heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
        /> is negotiable the moment it cannibalizes ad targeting. Under social capitalism,
        those primitives remain stable because the steward is bound by law and by
        non-profit governance, and because the institution is not engineered to
        maximize shareholder value—not because the open Internet technically
        prevents reversal. Lawful compliance may require adaptation of any
        feature; that is true of every platform operating within the law. The difference
        is what else can trigger those reversals. Under pure capitalism, direct communication,
        local-only processing, and every other privacy primitive are features on loan—revocable
        when revenue demands it, not only when regulation does. Under social capitalism,
        the steward operates fully within regulation but holds a higher ethical boundary
        than a for-profit company: capital gain alone is never sufficient reason to
        abandon a primitive or sell out the users who depend on it.
      </p>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-5" number={5} title="Companies Second">
        <p>
          Social media should be for people first, and companies are simply
          guests in the conversation.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can look to the physical world for a healthier model of corporate
          participation. When a company wants a presence at a major human social
          event—such as a cultural festival, a city marathon, or a community
          gathering—that presence is not granted by default, nor do they get to
          own the venue. Instead, their presence is usually linked to a massive
          donation or financial contribution to support the event itself. They
          are acknowledged as sponsors and guests, permitted to participate only
          because they are helping to sustain the space for the people actually
          attending.
        </p>

        <p>
          The digital town square should operate on the exact same principle.
          Companies should not dictate the architecture, own the personal data,
          or dictate what users see in their feeds. If a brand wishes to
          participate in a human social network, their presence should be
          treated as a privilege, contingent on contributing to the open-source
          maintenance of the platform or the non-profit organization stewarding
          it. By relegating corporations to the status of contributing guests
          rather than architectural overlords, social media can finally
          prioritize the human connections it was built to foster.
        </p>

        <p>
          Under social capitalism, a brand's presence in that town square is a
          privilege earned by supporting the commons—through open-source
          maintenance, contributions to the non-profit stewarding the
          platform—not a right purchased with ad spend. That is the rebalancing:
          people first, companies second.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-vi" part="Part VI">
        Law, Not Policy — Free Speech and Platform Responsibility
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-vi-platforms-as-private-legislatures">
        Platforms as Private Legislatures
      </H3>
      <p>
        Commercial social media platforms do not merely host speech—they govern
        it. Meta, X, and their peers maintain sprawling Terms of Service and
        "community standards" that function as a private legal code: vague,
        unilateral, and subject to change without notice. A user can be suspended,
        shadowbanned, or permanently removed not because they broke the law of any
        country, but because a trust-and-safety team decided their speech was
        "harmful," "misleading," or simply inconvenient to advertisers, regulators,
        or the platform's own leadership.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not neutral infrastructure. It is vigilante governance. The
        platform acts as prosecutor, judge, and executioner—with no obligation to
        publish evidence, hold a hearing, or submit its decision to independent
        review. When punishment is invisible, as with shadowbans, the user may not
        even know they have been sanctioned. The digital town square has been
        placed under the jurisdiction of corporate policy, not public law.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-vi-vigilante-enforcement-and-government-flagging">
        Vigilante Enforcement and Government Flagging
      </H3>
      <p>
        The problem deepens when informal state power routes through these private
        pipelines. Internal files released from X (formerly Twitter) documented
        formal channels between platform staff and U.S. government agencies—cases
        in which employees searched for reasons to suspend accounts that had been
        flagged, with no judicial order and no appeal. Litigation surrounding <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-411_19m2.pdf"
          >Murthy v. Missouri</ExternalLink
        > described similar pressure on Meta and other platforms to act on
        COVID-related speech, election content, and other politically sensitive
        topics. Whether or not every allegation in those cases constitutes unlawful
        coercion, the structural pattern is clear: governments can influence
        takedowns without going to court, and platforms can comply without
        disclosing that state influence shaped the outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        As documented in <InternalLink
          href="#part-vii-government-flagging-without-due-process-x"
          paper={sp}
          section={spSection('part-vii')}
          heading="Government Flagging Without Due Process — X"
        />, exposure followed—but no structural requirement to provide due process
        or to separate lawful compliance from discretionary enforcement. Users are
        left negotiating with entities whose moderation choices are opaque,
        revocable, and accountable to neither voters nor courts.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-vi-jurisdiction-follows-the-speaker">
        Jurisdiction Follows the Speaker
      </H3>
      <p>
        There is a healthier model, and maritime law offers a useful analogy. On
        the high seas, a vessel is primarily governed by the law of its flag state—the
        country of registration. In port, coastal states may enforce their own laws
        within territorial waters. The ship's owner does not write a parallel code
        of conduct for everyone aboard.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same principle should govern speech on a human social network. What a
        person may say should be bound by the law that governs <em>them</em>—where
        they reside, and where they are physically present—not by a platform's
        commercial interests or ideological preferences. The platform should not impose
        additional speech rules beyond applicable law. Users are not subject to a
        corporate morality layer sitting on top of statute.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is a deliberate rejection of the status quo. Meta and X can remove you
        because they dislike you, disagree with you, or because a government agency
        asked—often with no judge involved. On a network built for human welfare,
        the operative question is not "does the platform approve?" but "does
        applicable law prohibit this, and has due process been followed?"
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-vi-architecture-is-not-speech-policy">
        Architecture Is Not Speech Policy
      </H3>
      <p>
        "No additional speech rules" does not mean "no rules." It means
        distinguishing <em>speech governance</em> from <em>venue architecture</em>.
        The obligations described elsewhere in this paper—verified identity and one
        human per account (<InternalLink href="#part-iv" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-iv')} />), chronological feeds by default (<InternalLink href="#part-ii" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-ii')} />), guardrails for minor accounts (<InternalLink href="#part-iii" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-iii')} />)—are structural choices about how the space works. They are not a parallel speech code. A user in Germany and a user in Brazil may both post whatever their respective applicable laws permit; the platform does not rank lawful speech for outrage, bury it through shadowbans, or suppress it to protect advertiser comfort.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-vi-professional-advice-and-verified-credentials">
        Professional Advice and Verified Credentials
      </H3>
      <p>
        Not all speech on a social network is the same. General expression—opinions,
        debate, education—is one category. <em>Professional advice</em> is another:
        directive counsel that can cause serious harm when wrong, such as telling
        someone to take a medication, sell their shares, or sign a legal document.
        That is not ordinary opinion. It is regulated conduct carried out through words.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unauthorized practice of medicine, law, and regulated financial advice is
        already illegal in most jurisdictions. The platform should not judge whether
        professional advice is <em>correct</em>. It should require proof of license
        before a user may hold themselves out as a physician, lawyer, or regulated
        financial adviser. That is not a corporate morality layer—it is architecture
        aligned with law.
      </p>

      <p>
        Professional licenses map to <U>Electronic Attestations of Attributes</U> (EAA)
        and <U>Qualified Electronic Attestations of Attributes</U> (QEAA) under <ExternalLink
          href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1183"
          >eIDAS 2.0</ExternalLink
        >, issued by authentic sources—medical councils, bar associations, financial
        regulators—and presented through the <ExternalLink
          href="https://commission.europa.eu/topics/digital-economy-and-society/european-digital-identity_en"
          >EUDI Wallet</ExternalLink
        >. Users prove license type, jurisdiction, and validity through <InternalLink
          href="/technical-paper#part-i-selective-disclosure-and-zkp"
          paper="Technical Paper"
          section="Part I: Identity Verification Architecture"
          heading="Selective Disclosure and Zero-Knowledge Proofs"
        />—without oversharing identity. The platform verifies that a credential exists
        and is in scope; it does not endorse the advice itself.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Default speech:</U> Any verified human may post opinions and general information
          without a professional credential.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Professional advice mode:</U> Posting directive medical, legal, or regulated
          financial advice should require a verified credential. The account should display
          <em>Verified physician — [jurisdiction]</em> (or equivalent), not platform
          approval of the content.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Public disclaimers:</U> Professional posts on the public lane should carry
          a default notice that no doctor–patient, lawyer–client, or advisory relationship
          is created.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Jurisdiction:</U> A credential proves authority in its issuing jurisdiction.
          A license in one country does not automatically authorize practice elsewhere.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Bad advice from a licensed professional remains a matter for regulators,
        professional bodies, and courts—not for platform trust-and-safety teams.
        The EU credential ecosystem is still maturing; identity and professional frameworks
        beyond the European Union will be integrated as they mature.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-vi-what-the-platform-is-responsible-for">
        What the Platform Is Responsible For
      </H3>
      <p>
        Platform responsibility, under this model, is narrow and explicit:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Lawful compliance:</U> Where legislators have acted—as with minor-protection
          laws in <ExternalLink href="https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2024A00127/asmade/text"
            >Australia</ExternalLink
          >, the <ExternalLink href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/50/contents"
            >UK</ExternalLink
          >, and elsewhere—the platform complies fully. Disagreement with a policy
          is not permission to disregard it.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Lawful process:</U> Compelled removal, restriction, or deanonymization
          requires a valid legal obligation—court order, statutory duty, or competent
          authority—not an informal government flag or an internal policy judgment.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Transparency:</U> Government requests and legally compelled actions are
          logged and reported. State influence on enforcement is not hidden behind
          a Terms of Service pretext.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>No shadowbans:</U> If content is limited, the limit is visible to the
          user, attributable to a legal basis, and subject to the appeals process
          that applicable law requires.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>No discretionary ideological enforcement:</U> The platform does not
          police lawful speech for "brand safety," political convenience, or
          engagement optimization.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Cross-border speech remains hard. Content legal for the speaker may be
        illegal where it is accessed. Honest systems must confront audience
        jurisdiction—through technical geo-restriction where law requires it, not
        through global corporate censorship. VPNs, conflicts of law, and markets with
        incompatible speech regimes are limitations we acknowledge rather than
        paper over with a universal community standard.
      </p>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-6" number={6} title="Law, Not Terms of Service">
        <p>
          Free speech on the social web has been privatized. Platforms legislate,
          punish, and reverse their own rules at will—sometimes at the informal
          direction of governments, always without the constraints that bind public
          institutions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The platform should refuse that model. Speech is governed by law—the law that governs
          the speaker—not by corporate policy. The steward's duty is to build a
          trustworthy venue, comply with lawful process, and stay out of the business
          of moral censorship. Architecture protects people; credentials anchor professional
          accountability; law bounds speech; the platform is neither legislature nor vigilante.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-vii" part="Part VII">
        Lack of Accountability and Ethics in the Corporate Space
      </H2>

      <p>
        Under pure capitalism, ethical obligations—to users for honesty and
        safety, to advertisers for fair metrics, to regulators for truthful
        disclosure—are subordinated the moment they threaten revenue. Major
        platforms routinely promise privacy, safety, or control—and when
        incentives shift, they reverse course. When the law catches up, the bill
        can be real: billion-dollar fines, class actions, congressional
        hearings. Yet the cold, calculating math that governs big corporations
        often budgets those costs as needed taxes—the predictable line item of
        operating at scale—not a reason to change how the product works. Paying
        a fine does not restore an ethical obligation to keep one's word or
        protect vulnerable users. When fines are seen as taxes, breaking the
        law is treated as a privilege for those who can pay, not a line that
        must not be crossed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pattern repeats across companies and product lines. What follows is
        a list of documented examples—each with sources—of ethical breaches:
        deception, broken pledges, duty-of-care denial, misleading
        regulators—where neither moral reckoning nor structural consequence
        followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        <U>The WhatsApp Acquisition Deception — Meta</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Zuckerberg pledged at the 2014 acquisition not to change how WhatsApp
          uses data; in 2016 WhatsApp shared phone numbers and metadata with
          Facebook for advertising—new users had no opt-out. The FTC had warned
          before the deal; advocates filed complaints. No meaningful penalty
          followed (<ExternalLink
            href="https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_statements/297701/140410facebookwhatappltr.pdf"
            >FTC</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://abcnews.go.com/Business/Technology/whatsapp-sharing-data-facebook-raises-alarm-privacy-advocates/story?id=41717305"
            >ABC News</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2016/08/what-facebook-and-whatsapps-data-sharing-plans-really-mean-user-privacy-0"
            >EFF</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>The “Potential Reach” Scam — Meta</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Facebook’s Ads Manager presented “Potential Reach” as <em>people</em>
          advertisers could target; analysts found roughly 25 million more young
          Americans than Census data showed existed. Plaintiffs allege the
          metric counted accounts, not people. A class action remains active; the
          metric still ships and the ad business grew (<ExternalLink
            href="https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/06/business/media/facebook-advertisers.html"
            >The New York Times</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/meta-platforms-must-face-advertisers-class-action-us-appeals-court-says-2024-03-21/"
            >Reuters</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1326801/000162828026003942/R20.htm"
            >Meta 10-K</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>The “Off-Facebook” Tracking Settlement — Meta</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Zuckerberg’s <em>Clear History</em> promised to delete off-platform
          data; the shipped tool disconnects it from your profile but does not
          stop collection. The FTC imposed a $5 billion penalty in 2019; a $90
          million settlement covered only 2010–2011 Like-button tracking.
          Off-platform data remained central to ads (<ExternalLink
            href="https://about.fb.com/news/2018/05/clear-history-2/"
            >Meta</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2019/07/ftc-imposes-5-billion-penalty-sweeping-new-privacy-restrictions-facebook"
            >FTC</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.technologyreview.com/2020/01/29/276020/facebook-has-finally-launched-its-clear-history-button-but-it-doesnt-delete-anything/"
            >MIT Technology Review</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/transactional/facebook-90-mln-privacy-settlement-approved-over-antitrust-lawyers-objection-2022-11-14/"
            >Reuters</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>The 2019 Privacy Pivot — Meta</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Zuckerberg pledged “the future is private”—end-to-end encryption across
          Messenger, WhatsApp, and Instagram. Off-platform tracking continued;
          Instagram encryption stalled as opt-in only, then was removed. The
          pledge was treated as a marketing cycle, not a binding commitment (<ExternalLink
            href="https://apnews.com/article/north-america-ap-top-news-facebook-facebook-privacy-scandal--mark-zuckerberg-a510374d496d4fea8dd69abf4096fd6b"
            >AP</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.computerweekly.com/feature/Facebooks-privacy-U-turn-how-Zuckerberg-backtracked-on-promises-to-protect-personal-data"
            >Computer Weekly</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>The Teen Mental Health Denial — Meta</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Executives publicly called harms inconclusive; under oath, Zuckerberg
          denied a proven causal link to worse youth outcomes. Internal research
          found Instagram worsened body image for one in three teen girls;
          senators who requested the studies received a letter without them.
          Disclosures and lawsuits followed—no retreat from teen engagement (<ExternalLink
            href="https://docs.house.gov/meetings/IF/IF16/20211201/114268/HHRG-117-IF16-20211201-SD010.pdf"
            >House document</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink href="https://metasinternalresearch.org/"
            >Meta internal research summary</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.apollothirteen.com/article/court-papers-meta-halted-research-after-harm/"
            >litigation reporting</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>Instagram E2EE Buried in Settings, Then Removed — Instagram</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Default E2EE on Instagram DMs was promised; in 2023 it shipped as
          opt-in per chat, buried in settings. On 8 May 2026 Meta removed it,
          citing low adoption, and directed users to WhatsApp while rolling out
          Messenger ad hooks. Low adoption was manufactured; DMs are readable by
          Meta again (<ExternalLink
            href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clypzxl3lvqo"
            >BBC</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2026/05/broken-promises-rip-instagrams-end-end-encrypted-dms"
            >EFF</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/894752/instagram-end-to-end-encryption"
            >The Verge</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/messenger-platform/ads/ads-welcome-message-flows/"
            >Messenger ad hooks</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>Fake Secure Messaging — Telegram</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Telegram markets itself as a privacy-focused secure messenger, but
          default chats are cloud-stored—not end-to-end encrypted. Only optional
          Secret Chats are E2EE. Hundreds of millions of users treat it as
          private; no major penalty for the architecture (<ExternalLink
            href="https://www.telegram.org/press?setln=en"
            >Telegram press</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://freedom.press/digisec/blog/telegram-misrepresented-amid-founders-arrest/"
            >Freedom of the Press Foundation</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <U>Misleading Regulators on Security and Spam Bots — X</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Twitter claimed fewer than 5% of users were spam and represented FTC
          security compliance. Whistleblower Peiter Zatko alleged executives
          misled regulators, bonused growth over spam reduction, and avoided
          measuring true bot prevalence. Twitter denied it; a separate $150
          million FTC fine addressed phone-number ads, not bot metrics (<ExternalLink
            href="https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/interactive/2022/twitter-whistleblower-sec-spam/"
            >The Washington Post</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://apnews.com/article/technology-federal-trade-commission-us-securities-and-exchange-f1fe41b0bd7c5c407ecfb83509c671f5"
            >AP</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink
            href="https://www.cnbc.com/2022/08/23/twitter-whistleblower-alleges-execs-misled-board-and-public-on-spam-security.html"
            >CNBC</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p id="part-vii-government-flagging-without-due-process-x">
        <U>Government Flagging Without Due Process — X</U>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Twitter presented itself as an independent enforcer of its own Terms of
          Service. Internal files show formal pipelines with U.S. agencies; staff
          in documented cases searched for reasons to suspend flagged accounts
          with no hearing or appeal. Exposure followed, but no structural
          requirement to disclose state influence or provide due process (<ExternalLink
            href="https://twitterfiles.substack.com/p/twitter-the-fbi-subsidiary"
            >Matt Taibbi</ExternalLink
          >, <ExternalLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter_Files"
            >summary</ExternalLink
          >).
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Across these platforms, the arc is the same: promise trust,
        misstate or bury the control, and—when the law catches up—pay for the
        past. Then keep operating. The math is cold and deliberate: eat the
        fine, retain the architecture, repeat. Reversals are product
        decisions—not betrayals with remedies that bind the next promise. Until
        penalties force lasting change, ethical obligations—honesty, duty of
        care, fair dealing—and legal accountability remain optional for
        corporations large enough to price the bill. Deception is normalized
        when profitable.
      </p>

      <Conclusion
        id="conclusion-7"
        number={7}
        title="When Fines Are Not Accountability—or Ethics"
      >
        <p>
          The examples above share one pattern: corporations say what users,
          advertisers, and regulators want to hear; do something else when
          revenue, regulation, or product strategy shifts; and, when the law
          catches up, pay a real price—fines, settlements, lawsuits, and public
          hearings. Those costs are not imaginary. Too often they are absorbed
          through the same cold, calculating math: fines as a tax on revenue, the
          cost of doing business until the next reversal. That is not
          accountability—and it is not ethics. Settling a lawsuit is not the
          same as accepting a moral obligation or changing architecture. Paying a
          penalty addresses legality; it does not bind the next product decision
          or restore a duty to tell the truth.
        </p>

        <p>
          Users, advertisers, and regulators are left negotiating with entities
          whose core incentive is revenue, not truth. That gap is an ethical
          failure as much as a governance one—structural, not the mistake of a
          single executive or product team. A corporation that treats its public
          commitments as marketing copy cannot be trusted to police itself;
          self-policing cannot substitute for duties owed to the people who
          depend on the product. The next reversal is already priced in.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-viii" part="Part VIII">The Blueprint for a Private Web</H2>

      <H3 id="part-viii-synthesizing-the-vision">Synthesizing the Vision</H3>
      <p>
        The trajectory of the modern Internet is not an accident; it is the
        mathematical result of an open architecture colliding with the absolute
        demand for capital gains. To repair the digital town square, we cannot
        rely on the very corporations that profit from its degradation. We must
        address all the systemic failures concurrently:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must improve the Web, as described in <InternalLink
            href="#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
            paper={sp}
            section={spSection('part-i')}
            heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
          />, by embedding local-only privacy primitives (like local-only feed building,
          secure cross-device synchronization, and encrypted backups) directly into
          its DNA.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must add the "social" back into social media, as described in <InternalLink href="#part-ii" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-ii')} />, by restoring users' benefit over the feed—reverse-chronological by default,
          with local-only algorithms available only as an explicit choice—rather than
          forcing outrage-optimized feeds designed for capital gains.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must protect the vulnerable, as described in <InternalLink href="#part-iii" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-iii')} />, by designing spaces that safely ease children into the digital world, rather
          than exploiting their developing minds.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must enforce a people first, bots never hierarchy, as described in <InternalLink href="#part-iv" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-iv')} />, by utilizing cryptographic identity protocols to eradicate
          propaganda, misinformation, disinformation, malinformation, and fake news,
          social media farms, and coordinated multi-account manipulation—and to enforce
          human accountability.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must put companies second, as described in <InternalLink href="#part-v" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-v')} />, ensuring that corporate entities are treated as contributing guests rather
          than algorithmic dictators.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must refuse corporate speech governance, as described in <InternalLink
            href="#part-vi"
            paper={sp}
            section={spSection('part-vi')}
          />, by binding what users may say to applicable law—not to platform Terms of
          Service, shadowbans, or informal government flagging without judicial
          oversight.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must gate professional advice behind verified licenses, as described in <InternalLink
            href="#part-vi-professional-advice-and-verified-credentials"
            paper={sp}
            section={spSection('part-vi')}
            heading="Professional Advice and Verified Credentials"
          />, using the EUDI Wallet to prove medical, legal, or regulated financial qualifications—without
          the platform judging the truth of the advice itself.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must insist on corporate accountability and ethics, as described in <InternalLink href="#part-vii" paper={sp} section={spSection('part-vii')} />, by recognizing that commercial platforms can deceive users and
          regulators, deny duty of care, treat fines as a tax—pay, keep
          operating, break promises, enforce bans without due process—and face
          no obligation, ethical or structural, to keep their word.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-final" final title="The Commitment to Build">
        <p>
          Identifying these problems is no longer sufficient. Writing about the
          necessary architecture is only the first step; the final step is to
          build it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Honest work remains on several fronts: age verification for minors is
          still in progress; a sustainable funding model for a non-profit
          platform must be proven in practice; and identity frameworks beyond
          the European Union will be integrated as they mature. This paper
          states the principles. The build will test them.
        </p>

        <p>
          To execute this vision, we are officially establishing a public and
          transparent non-profit Non-Governmental Organization (NGO). The sole
          mission of this organization will be to build, steward, and maintain a
          fundamentally new, open-source social media platform. In the beginning,
          this organization will be funded by donations and grants—not venture
          capital chasing a network-effects exit.
        </p>

        <p>
          This platform will be engineered from the ground up with the
          uncompromisable <InternalLink
            href="#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
            paper={sp}
            section={spSection('part-i')}
            heading="The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives"
          />. By operating as an NGO, we are permanently removing the structural need
          to maximize shareholder value. The code will be open, the algorithms
          will be transparent, and the computation of intent will remain
          strictly on your local devices.
        </p>

        <p>
          We are not building a platform to harvest your data, hook your
          attention, or optimize for outrage. We are building it entirely with
          the user's benefit in mind, deliberately rejecting the pursuit of
          "capital gains" that has corrupted the digital landscape. It is time
          to reclaim the digital town square and build a network that truly
          works for humanity.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>
  </Article>
